etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4176079\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: bitcoinist.com\nSLUG: the-higher-bitcoin-goes-the-less-institutions-want-it-coinbase-executive-weighs-in\nTEXT: Coinbase\'s John D’Agostino says institutions are buying Bitcoin at lower prices, despite the recent drop to around $60,000. In a CNBC interview, D’Agostino notes that institutions have spent years studying Bitcoin and tend to grow more interested as prices fall, not less. Family offices, sovereign wealth funds, and government investment entities in the Middle East are among those viewing the recent correction as a chance to buy at lower prices. Despite the drop, investors have kept over $750 billion in exposure through spot Bitcoin ETFs. D’Agostino argues that price swings are normal for an asset class that behaves like a commodity, and the improved market infrastructure and evolving regulatory framework make the current environment stronger than previous downturns.\n\nID: 4173045\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: forehead-tattoos-and-alcohol-dares-inside-the-dark-underbelly-of-crypto-s-memecoin-craze\nTEXT: Bitcoin\'s recent rebound to $63,000 may be short-lived if May CPI inflation data reveals rising prices. Since the April U.S. CPI report, $5.4 billion in net redemptions have been seen in U.S.-listed bitcoin ETFs, driven by institutional selling. 10x Research\'s Markus Thielen argues that Bitcoin\'s selloff below $60,000 was driven by institutional selling through spot bitcoin ETFs after reaccelerating inflation data.\n\n\n',)
