etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4176376\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: reason.com\nSLUG: trumps-100000-h-1b-visa-fee-is-an-unconstitutional-tax-a-federal-judge-rules\nTEXT: A federal judge has ruled that President Trump\'s $100,000 visa fee for H-1B visas is an unconstitutional tax. Judge Sorokin\'s decision vacates the visa fee, contrasting with a December ruling by Judge Beryl Howell that justified the fee as part of the President\'s broad authority to regulate entry into the US. The disagreement hinges on statutory interpretation and a constitutional question: whether Trump\'s fee usurped Congress\' tax power. The H-1B program, created in 1990, allows employers to hire foreign workers for jobs requiring specialized knowledge. The program is capped at 85,000 visas a year, and employers previously paid up to $5,000 for each visa. Trump\'s proclamation prohibited entry under H-1B visas except for those accompanied by a $100,000 payment.\n\nID: 4173186\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 667825-trumps-100000-h-1b-visa-fee-declared-unlawful\nTEXT: A federal judge in Boston has declared a $100,000 fee imposed by US President Donald Trump on new H-1B visas for highly skilled foreign workers unlawful. The fee, which was announced in September and raised the cost of obtaining H-1B visas dramatically, was challenged by 20 Democratic state attorneys general in a lawsuit. US District Judge Leo Sorokin ruled that the fee constituted an unlawful tax, not a monetary penalty, and that Trump lacked authorization from Congress to impose it. As a result, the fee is now deemed unlawful and employers seeking H-1B visas for foreign workers will no longer have to pay it.\n\nID: 4173006\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71526367\nTEXT: A federal judge, District Judge Leo Sorokin, has voided President Donald Trump\'s $100,000 application fee requirement for H-1B visas. The judge ruled that only Congress had the power to change federal immigration policy, which he viewed as a tax. Sorokin rejected arguments from the administration that the president had the power to implement the requirement due to federal immigration law giving him leeway to change U.S. policy in other ways. The judge\'s 42-page decision struck down the policy, which was imposed several months after a coalition of Democratic state attorneys general brought the case.\n\nID: 4176271\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: emirates247.com\nSLUG: 2399\nTEXT: A US judge has struck down a $10,000 H-1B visa fee introduced by the Trump administration. The ruling sided with a coalition of 20 states, finding that the policy imposed a tax without proper authorisation from Congress. The fee was part of efforts to limit the hiring of foreign workers and prioritise American jobs, but critics argued it made it more difficult to fill essential roles such as doctors and teachers. The decision marks a significant setback for the administration\'s efforts to restrict visa access through financial measures, and broader immigration policies remain under scrutiny.\n\nID: 4172921\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: khou.com\nSLUG: 507-ed6d05c3-d5ef-4e23-b0a2-b7b29336bf3d\nTEXT: A federal judge has struck down Trump\'s fee for H-1B visas, which were set to take effect on July 10. The judge ruled that the fee increase was unlawful, citing a 2019 court decision that prohibited the government from charging a fee for H-1B petitions. The Trump administration had argued that the fee increase was necessary to offset the costs of processing H-1B petitions. The ruling affects over 85,000 foreign workers who rely on the H-1B visa program to work in the US.\n\nID: 4173027\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: thenews.com.pk\nSLUG: 1405245-trumps-100000-h-1b-visa-fee-is-unlawful\nTEXT: A federal U.S. judge, Leo Sorokin, has struck down a $100,000 fee imposed by President Donald Trump on new H-1B visas for highly skilled foreign workers. The ruling, issued in Boston, is a result of a lawsuit filed by 20 Democratic state attorneys general. The fee, announced in September, was deemed an unlawful tax that Congress never authorized.\n\n\n',)
