etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4175423\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: china-companies-us-blacklist-opposed-6170961\nTEXT: The US Defence Department has released a list of 80 Chinese companies and their subsidiaries, including tech giants Alibaba, Baidu, and Tencent, which are targeted for potential sanctions. The list is largely similar to the one published in February, with two memory chipmakers, ChangXin Memory Technologies and Yangtze Memory Technologies, being reinstated. The companies have expressed opposition to their inclusion on the list, with Alibaba calling it a "mistake" and Baidu stating there is no credible justification for adding it. The move is seen as a test of relations between the US and China, with President Trump inviting Chinese counterpart Xi Jinping to visit Washington in September.\n\nID: 4175646\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: pentagon-blacklist-raises-spectre-investment-curbs-chinese-tech-firms\nTEXT: The Pentagon\'s expanding blacklist of Chinese companies is raising concerns about reputational risks for tech firms like Alibaba and Baidu, which have been designated as "Chinese military companies" under Section 1260H of the National Defence Authorisation Act. The latest additions include companies like Alibaba, Baidu, BYD, WuXi AppTec, RoboSense and Unitree Robotics, which span sectors such as artificial intelligence, electric vehicles, robotics and biotechnology. ChangXin Memory Technologies (CXMT) and Yangtze Memory Technologies Co (YMTC), Chinese memory-chip makers, have also been added to the blacklist.\n\nID: 4175721\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: fakti.bg\nSLUG: 1059973-china-us-to-immediately-correct-its-mistake\nTEXT: China has called on the US to correct its decision to include Chinese companies, such as Alibaba and BYD, on a list of enterprises linked to the Chinese military. The Global Times reported that Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Lin Jian stated, "China has consistently opposed the US\'s overly broad interpretation of national security and discriminatory lists." Lin Jian also warned that Beijing will take necessary measures to protect the rights and interests of its companies, citing "groundless pressure on Chinese companies".\n\nID: 4175900\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: alibaba-byd-baidu-added-pentagon-blacklist-over-alleged-china-military-ties-87649\nTEXT: The US has added Alibaba, Baidu, and BYD to its list of companies believed to be aiding China\'s military development. This move comes amid ongoing tensions between the US and China over trade, technology, and national security. The inclusion on the Pentagon\'s list does not constitute sanctions, but analysts warn it can affect investor confidence and future regulatory actions. The designation joins a growing list of Chinese firms facing heightened scrutiny, including those in the electric vehicle and AI sectors.\n\nID: 4175890\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: hurriyetdailynews.com\nSLUG: pentagon-says-alibaba-baidu-and-other-tech-firms-aiding-chinas-military-223070\nTEXT: The Pentagon has released a list of Chinese military companies, including Alibaba, Baidu, and Tencent, which are aiding China\'s military. Representative John Moolenaar warned American businesses and the government to stop doing business with these companies, citing them as threats to national security. He urged U.S. companies to "stop doing business with these threats" or risk enabling China\'s military ascendance, highlighting the involvement of key tech giants in artificial intelligence.\n\nID: 4173258\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: thenextweb.com\nSLUG: pentagon-1260h-alibaba-baidu-byd-unitree-chinese-military\nTEXT: The US is using a list of Chinese tech companies, including Alibaba and Baidu, to pressure China. The 1260H list is one of several mechanisms the US uses to restrict Chinese tech, alongside export controls on semiconductor equipment and tariffs. The Commerce Department\'s Bureau of Industry and Security maintains the Entity List, which includes companies like Alibaba and Baidu.\n\n\n',)
