etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4175438\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: apnews.com\nSLUG: ice-immigration-crackdown-texas-camp-montana-report-04bc547c02e7241fc73541a4d0ba26ad\nTEXT: The US government wasted millions of dollars on a detention facility in Montana, known as Camp East Montana. The $1.3 billion deal was awarded to Acquisition Logistics, a small contractor with no prior experience operating detention facilities. The facility failed to meet ICE detention standards and had numerous security blind spots, including a lack of security cameras on the perimeter. Detainees faced substandard care due to missing evidence, unsanitary conditions, and inadequate medical assessments. The facility also failed to provide essential services such as recreation areas, law libraries, and showers compliant with the Americans with Disabilities Act. The problems persisted despite the facility\'s average population being around 2,500, half of its intended capacity.\n\nID: 4175998\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71538665\nTEXT: The US government\'s General Accountability Office (GAO) has found that the contractor managing Camp East Montana, the nation\'s largest immigration detention facility, wasted millions of dollars and put detainees at risk due to inadequate medical care and screening. The GAO\'s report highlights previously undisclosed incidents, including a detainee escaping in October due to the contractor\'s oversight failure. In January, a loaded firearm was lost by a security guard inside the facility, which was never recovered. The contractor also failed to administer skin tests for tuberculosis, relying on a questionnaire instead, allowing a detainee with the disease to be housed with the general population and leading to an outbreak.\n\n\n',)
