etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4172995\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71525483\nTEXT: Brendan Sorsby, a 22-year-old quarterback at Texas Tech, has been granted a temporary injunction against the NCAA, allowing him to potentially play for the team this fall. The injunction was granted after Sorsby\'s lawsuit, filed May 18, sought the restoration of his eligibility. The NCAA had previously denied Sorsby\'s request to reinstate his eligibility, citing a betting scandal involving the quarterback. Judge Ken Curry ruled that the NCAA cannot block Sorsby\'s eligibility, citing a diagnosed addiction and anxiety-driven compulsion. This decision could clear the way for Sorsby to play for Texas Tech, despite being declared ineligible due to wagering on college sports.\n\nID: 4175809\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: boston25news.com\nSLUG: l3szuuvmf5gkro43fcuweu26qu\nTEXT: A judge has ruled that Texas Tech QB Brendan Sorsby is eligible to play despite an NCAA ban on gambling. The decision came after Sorsby admitted to thousands of bets, including 40 on his own team while playing at Indiana. Sorsby will miss the Red Raiders\' first two games of the 2026 season under a penalty proposed by his attorneys and approved by the judge. The NCAA strongly disagrees with the ruling, citing concerns about the integrity of sports and plans to file an appeal. Reaction from several schools and conferences has been negative, with ACC commissioner Jim Phillips calling it a "horrendous pattern" that is eroding the integrity of the process.\n\nID: 4173152\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: deseret.com\nSLUG: brendan-sorsby-gets-injunction-vs-ncaa-could-play-for-texas-tech-this-fall\nTEXT: Brendan Sorsby, a 22-year-old quarterback, has received an injunction in his lawsuit against the NCAA. The case centers on Sorsby\'s eligibility to play for Texas Tech this fall, following an NCAA investigation. Sorsby has a diagnosed addiction and anxiety-driven compulsion, which his attorney, Jeffrey Kessler, claims would be exacerbated by not playing football. In contrast, the NCAA maintains that it must defend against actions that defraud college athletics and threaten competitive integrity. The injunction allows Sorsby to play for Texas Tech this fall, pending further appeals by the NCAA.\n\nID: 4173069\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: deseret.com\nSLUG: brendan-sorsby-texas-tech-ruling-eligibility-big-12-byu-utah-ncaa-texas-judge\nTEXT: Texas Tech quarterback Brendan Sorsby has won a temporary restraining order against the NCAA, allowing him to transfer from Cincinnati and play for the Red Raiders this season despite admitting to a gambling addiction. Local Texas Judge Ken Curry granted Sorsby\'s injunction request, sparking shockwaves in the Big 12 and questioning the NCAA\'s power over its member schools. The ruling has raised concerns about playing Texas Tech in any sports this season, with Kansas State athletic director Gene Taylor stating that the Wildcats have had "serious conversation" about it.\n\nID: 4176195\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: deseret.com\nSLUG: is-anyone-really-looking-out-for-brendan-sorsby-opinion\nTEXT: Brendan Sorsby\'s gambling story has sparked widespread criticism, with many people turning against Texas Tech and Sorsby. The issue revolves around a ruling by Texas district judge Ken Curry, who stated that Sorsby would suffer "a probable, imminent and irreparable injury" if he is not allowed to play football this season at Texas Tech. This ruling was made in response to the NCAA\'s attempt to ban Sorsby from playing due to his gambling activities.\n\n\n',)
