etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4173100\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: thestar.com.my\nSLUG: us-denies-entry-to-somali-referee-traveling-for-world-cup\nTEXT: The US has denied entry to a Somali referee, Artan, who was traveling for the World Cup. The US Customs and Border Patrol deemed him inadmissible due to vetting concerns, despite having a valid visa. Artan had arrived at Miami International Airport from Istanbul on Saturday and underwent routine additional inspection before being denied entry. The CBP stated that admissibility determinations are made on a case-by-case basis, but did not elaborate on the specific concerns. This decision comes amid concerns about the Trump administration\'s strict immigration policies, which have been a point of contention ahead of the World Cup.\n\nID: 4176228\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: thestar.com.my\nSLUG: soccer-somali-soccer-referee-on-his-way-back-home-after-being-denied-entry-into-united-states\nTEXT: Somali soccer referee, Abdi Artan, is on his way back home after being denied entry into the United States. His international achievements are a source of honor and pride for the Somali people, according to Somalia\'s sports ministry. Artan was denied entry due to vetting concerns and underwent a routine additional inspection before being deemed inadmissible. The Trump administration\'s travel ban on citizens of 12 countries, including Somalia, may be related to Artan\'s case. Despite having a valid visa, Artan will not be able to referee games in Mexico and Canada due to logistical and security reasons.\n\nID: 4175923\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: somali-referee-omar-artan-denied-u-s-entry-set-miss-historic-world-cup-debut-87682\nTEXT: Somali referee Omar Artan was denied entry into the United States ahead of the 2026 World Cup, forcing FIFA to remove him from the list of active referees for the tournament. Artan, a FIFA-listed referee since 2018, was selected to officiate at the expanded 48-team competition across the US, Canada and Mexico. His absence has sparked concern among football officials and observers, raising questions about how entry restrictions can affect FIFA-appointed personnel at a tournament hosted across multiple countries.\n\nID: 4175479\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: somalia-world-cup-football-referee-denied-us-entry-6170596\nTEXT: A Somali national, who was a World Cup football referee, was denied entry to the US due to vetting concerns. The individual arrived at Miami International Airport from Istanbul on Saturday and underwent a routine additional inspection, which led to his inadmissibility. The US Customs and Border Protection (CBP) declined to elaborate on the specific concerns, stating that admissibility determinations are made on a case-by-case basis using available law enforcement, national security, and immigration information.\n\nID: 4176010\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71536027\nTEXT: FIFA has dropped Somali referee Omar Artan from the World Cup after he was denied entry to the US. The decision comes after Artan\'s visa application was rejected by CBP officers, who have the authority to question travelers and conduct inspections. Despite being praised as one of Africa\'s best referees, Artan will not be able to train or officiate at the World Cup. In a statement, FIFA said Artan\'s status will not be changed and he would not be able to participate in the tournament.\n\nID: 4173079\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: wsvn.com\nSLUG: somali-referee-wont-officiate-in-world-cup-after-being-denied-entry-into-the-united-states\nTEXT: A Somali referee was denied entry into the United States due to vetting concerns, following an inspection by US Customs and Border Protection (CBP). The referee, who was set to officiate in the FIFA World Cup, was deemed inadmissible. CBP noted that all travelers seeking entry into the US are subject to inspection and vetting, which includes questioning, inspections, and admissibility determinations made on a case-by-case basis.\n\nID: 4173162\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 667854-us-denies-entry-to-somali-referee-traveling-for-world-cup\nTEXT: The US has denied entry to Somali referee Abdi Artan, who was set to travel for the World Cup finals in Canada, Mexico and the US. Artan, a FIFA referee since 2018, was among 52 referees announced by the organization to officiate at the World Cup. His denial is likely due to the Trump administration\'s strict immigration policies, which have been a point of concern for World Cup attendees.\n\nID: 4176203\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: globalnews.ca\nSLUG: somali-referee-denied-entry-to-us-world-cup\nTEXT: Somali referee denied entry to US World Cup. Artan, praised as one of Africa\'s best referees, was set to make history by officiating at the World Cup. However, he was denied entry to the US tournament, citing security concerns. Artan spoke about facing challenges in his conflict-torn country, including having to change his training route due to explosions in Mogadishu.\n\nID: 4175486\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: apnews.com\nSLUG: world-cup-referee-somalia-fifa-trump-04dc046d9807582d5b69e0149181e5f1\nTEXT: Somalia\'s World Cup referee, Artan, has faced challenges in his conflict-torn country, including explosions in Mogadishu that forced him to change his training route. Despite these obstacles, Artan remains focused on his refereeing career and is excited to make history as the first Somali to referee at the World Cup.\n\n\n',)
