etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4177449\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668023-iran-targets-us-bases-in-jordan-and-the-gulf-after-trump-orders-strikes-near-hormuz\nTEXT: US President Trump downplays a recent incident where a US Apache helicopter was brought down by an Iranian attack drone, saying "it wasn\'t a big deal" and that the pilot is fine. However, this comes as tensions escalate between the US and Iran, with Iranian forces targeting US bases in Jordan and the Gulf. The US has reportedly struck back, with nearly 20 Iranian targets hit, including the US al-Azraq base in Jordan and the Ali Al Salem base in Kuwait. Iran\'s Revolutionary Guards claim to have targeted four sites at the US al-Azraq base, including F-35 fighter jet hangars and a command-and-control centre. The Kuwaiti army has also reported engaging hostile aerial targets, while Bahrain\'s Interior Ministry sounded a warning siren and urged the public to head to safety. The US has imposed its own blockade of Iranian ports, while Iran demands the lifting of international sanctions and recognition of its control over the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nID: 4177406\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: gulftoday.ae\nSLUG: iran-fires-missiles-at-air-base-hosting-us-forces-in-jordan\nTEXT: Iran has fired missiles at an air base hosting US forces in Jordan, prompting air raid sirens to sound in Bahrain. The Kuwaiti military reported engaging "hostile aerial targets" with its air defences, without immediately mentioning the aggressor. This incident follows a US military statement that it had completed a retaliatory assault on Iran over the downing of an Apache attack helicopter. US Central Command (CENTCOM) said it had struck Iranian air defense, ground control stations, and surveillance radar sites near the Strait of Hormuz with precision munitions from US Air Force and Navy fighter jets. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi had earlier threatened payback, saying the US had opted to test Iran\'s determination.\n\nID: 4176905\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: thedailystar.net\nSLUG: us-strikes-iran-irgc-targets-us-base-jordan-4195081\nTEXT: The US has struck Iranian IRGC targets at a US base in Jordan, according to the General Staff of the Army. Kuwaiti air defence systems are engaging hostile aerial targets in accordance with established operational procedures, but the origin of the targets is not specified. The US-Iran conflict has disrupted shipping via the Strait of Hormuz, a critical oil passage, and Washington has imposed a blockade on Iranian ports. The US Army Apache helicopter was shot down by Iran during the war, but two crew members were rescued after their helicopter went down near the coast of Oman. The price of US oil jumped 1.4 percent after the news of renewed US strikes, reaching $89.40 per barrel.\n\nID: 4176346\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: scrippsnews.com\nSLUG: us-begins-new-self-defense-strikes-against-iran-in-response-to-apache-helicopter-incident\nTEXT: The US has launched new self-defense strikes against Iran in response to an Apache helicopter incident. The incident occurred on June 9, 2026, when an Iranian surface-to-air missile reportedly shot down a US Apache helicopter. This attack has strained the two-month-old ceasefire between the US and Iran, which was tested earlier on Monday when Iran reportedly struck targets in Israel. The White House has repeatedly signaled a deal to permanently end the conflict is close, with President Trump stating "We have a good chance" of signing a deal in "two or three days". However, Iranian representatives claim the US actions indicate the country is not seeking a ceasefire nor dialogue.\n\nID: 4176876\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: iran-us-bases-retaliatory-strikes-trump-hormuz-jordan-gulf-6173196\nTEXT: The US and Iran have exchanged retaliatory strikes, with the US targeting Iranian sites in the Strait of Hormuz. Almost 20 Iranian targets were struck, including Qeshm island and Sirik port city in the Strait of Hormuz. Iranian media reported sounds of explosions in nearby Bandar Abbas and Jask, near the entrance to the strait. The US al-Azraq base in Jordan was also targeted by Iranian Revolutionary Guards, who claimed to have hit F-35 fighter jet hangars and a command-and-control centre. Jordanian armed forces intercepted and shot down five missiles launched from Iran, with debris falling on Jordanian territory but causing no injuries or material damage.\n\nID: 4176877\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: new-strikes-iran-apache-helicopter-6171926\nTEXT: US President Trump stated that the response to Iran\'s Apache helicopter strike should be "very strong, very powerful". The strikes began at 5pm ET on Wednesday and ended just before 9pm ET, targeting Qeshm island, Sirik in the Strait of Hormuz, and nearby Bandar Abbas. US bases in the region were also targeted in response. Iranian media reported that the Revolutionary Guards attacked the US Fifth Fleet in Bahrain with drones, threatening "more severe responses" if hostilities continued. Oil prices rose about 1% in early Asian trade following the escalation. Two US pilots involved in the helicopter incident were uninjured, according to Trump.\n\nID: 4176016\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71539628\nTEXT: The US launched "self-defense" strikes against Iran on Wednesday, targeting a downed helicopter that was patrolling near the Strait of Hormuz. The strikes were in response to President Trump\'s claim that Iran was responsible for the helicopter\'s shooting down. CENTCOM stated that the mission is a proportional response to "unjustified Iranian aggression". Trump said the two pilots on board were safe and uninjured, but emphasized that the US must respond to this attack. The White House did not immediately comment on whether full-scale fighting would resume or if peace negotiations would continue.\n\nID: 4177273\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: tempo.co\nSLUG: us-strikes-iran-after-trump-blames-tehran-for-apache-crash\nTEXT: The US has launched strikes in Iran after President Trump blamed Tehran for the recent Apache helicopter crash. Tim Hawkins, a spokesperson for US Central Command, highlighted the growing role of autonomous systems in military operations. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi rejected Trump\'s accusations, stating that foreign forces near Iran\'s territory are at risk of accidents or confrontations. Araghchi warned that Iran would retaliate against the latest US attacks, saying "Iranian forces will leave no attack or threat unanswered."\n\nID: 4177445\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: emirates247.com\nSLUG: 2420\nTEXT: The US launched airstrikes on Iran after an Apache attack helicopter was lost, with Iranian state television reporting that the Israeli attacks killed at least two members of Iran\'s air-defense units. The strikes, conducted by US fighter jets from the Air Force and Navy, targeted "air defense, ground control stations, and surveillance radar sites" in Iran. The US military\'s Central Command confirmed the strikes, which occurred a day after Iran and Israel exchanged fire for the first time since the fragile ceasefire took effect.\n\nID: 4176190\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: wsvn.com\nSLUG: us-says-it-has-begun-strikes-against-iran-following-crash-of-army-apache-helicopter-off-oman-coast\nTEXT: The US has begun strikes against Iran following the crash of an Army Apache helicopter off Oman\'s coast. Iranian state television reported that Israeli attacks killed at least two members of Iran\'s air-defense units since the US and Israel started striking on February 28. The conflict has driven up energy prices globally, making basics like food more expensive. A drone boat rescued US service members picked up by a helicopter after it went down during a patrol off Oman\'s coast, and they were later picked up by another helicopter.\n\n\n',)
