etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4175915\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: kane-evans-comes-out-gay-ex-nrl-player-opens-about-secret-battle-addiction-mental-health-crisis-87650\nTEXT: Kane Evans, a former NRL player, has come out as gay and opened up about his secret battle with addiction and mental health crisis. Evans credits former NRL player Joe Galuvao, who works with the Rugby League Players Association\'s past players and transition program, for helping him seek treatment and begin rebuilding his life. Sydney Roosters coach Trent Robinson provided emotional and practical support during Evans\' recovery, reassuring him that the team remained his home. Evans recently celebrated over four months of sobriety and marked the beginning of a new chapter in his life with his public coming out. The announcement drew widespread support from across rugby league, including from Ian Roberts and Andrew Johns, who praised Evans\' courage.\n\nID: 4177035\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: smh.com.au\nSLUG: this-is-the-moment-trailblazer-ian-roberts-reacts-to-kane-evans-revelation-20260609-p6050i-html\nTEXT: The NRL has commended Kane Evans for his courage in coming out as gay, believing it will spark important conversations that can help others struggling with their sexuality. Evans, a 34-year-old Fijian international who played for the Roosters, Eels and Warriors, detailed his struggles with alcohol, substance addiction and suicidal thoughts while wrestling with his sexuality during an emotional interview on Channel Nine\'s 100% Footy. This marks the first time a male player has come out as gay in professional Australian rugby league since Ian Roberts 31 years ago. NRL chief executive Andrew Abdo praised Evans for being brave and telling his story, saying "Hopefully that helps him as a human being and inspires others as well."\n\n\n',)
