etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4176260\nCREATED: 2026-06-10\nDOMAIN: gizmodo.com\nSLUG: the-us-screwworm-outbreak-is-starting-to-look-harder-to-contain-2000769424\nTEXT: The US screwworm outbreak is starting to look harder to contain, with four cases identified in Texas so far. The USDA has reported the first local livestock case seen in decades, a four-month-old calf, and experts have been anticipating that screwworm cases would eventually show up in the US following the barrier breach. Dudley Hoskins, Under Secretary for Marketing and Regulatory Programs at USDA, stated that the agency is working closely with partners in New Mexico, Texas, and across the region to identify, contain, and respond to potential cases. The screwworm\'s maggot form prefers to feed on living flesh, causing extensive tissue damage and potentially life-threatening consequences. The USDA has estimated that similar outbreaks could cost the Texas economy $1.8 billion, and rising beef prices may be affected by the return of the screwworm.\n\nID: 4176008\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71532344\nTEXT: The screwworm, a flesh-eating parasite, has returned to US cattle after decades. The parasite, which was eradicated in the 1960s, has caused significant financial burdens on cattle producers. According to David Anderson, an agriculture economist at Texas A&M University, the outbreak would increase labor and medicine costs for ranchers. The US Department of Agriculture has confirmed at least three infected cattle in Texas, and the beef industry is already grappling with a small cattle herd due to prolonged droughts. The USDA has broken ground on a facility to mass produce sterile screwworm flies in Texas, with production expected to start in November 2027.\n\nID: 4176006\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71539378\nTEXT: A screwworm infestation is affecting the cattle industry in New Mexico, with the severity of the outbreak depending on the type and extent of remediation. The parasite, which feeds on living flesh, poses a significant threat to livestock and pets.\n\n\n',)
