etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4156552\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: hyperallergic.com\nSLUG: artist-julio-le-parc-maestro-of-light-movement-and-defiance-dies-at-97\nTEXT: Julio Le Parc, a renowned artist and master of the Light Movement and Defiance, has passed away at 97. Le Parc defied institutional expectations by canceling an exhibition after it landed on tails, showcasing his refusal to be absorbed by the institution at its peak. His work gained widespread acclaim with major retrospectives at the Pérez Art Museum Miami, Metropolitan Museum of Art, and an upcoming show at Tate Modern. Curator Estrellita B. Brodsky, who worked closely with Le Parc for decades, first met him three decades ago while working on her dissertation.\n\nID: 4173329\nCREATED: 2026-06-09\nDOMAIN: batimes.com.ar\nSLUG: tate-modern-exhibition-pays-tribute-to-julio-le-parc-days-after-death-phtml\nTEXT: The Tate Modern exhibition is paying tribute to Julio Le Parc, a young artist who played a crucial role in the creation of the Groupe de Recherche d\'Art Visuel (GRAV) in 1960. The works of Le Parc, which include his contributions to the development of installation art, reveal his central concern: challenging societal rules and limitations on artistic practice. According to Ravaglia, Le Parc\'s work is particularly significant in the context of immersive and interactive art, laying the foundations for how art is conceived in relation to the viewer.\n\n\n',)
